(1738838, 228978, 2667, '<td valign="top">You really did a great job with this image. Although I did not vote this challenge, I did look (I always look :) and this was one of the images that stood out. <br/> <br/>The good things:<br/> <br/>Olivia''s awesome features. Seriously, she has the look a professional model. Unusual and just a touch exotic with some truely striking features.<br/> <br/>Non centered composition. It would have been so easy to plunk her into the center of the frame... but you didn''t. Kudos on that choice.<br/> <br/>Guitar earrings. Playful, and at the same time they echo the shape of her face and jawline. <br/> <br/>The not so good:<br/> <br/>Sharpness is a double edged sword here. She is SO sharp (oversharp, in fact) that it is impossible not to comment on it. Portrait work is often suited to a mix of both sharpness and softness. You want sharpness on specific features, such as the eyes and lips, with softness on the others.<br/> <br/>Whether you actually used NI or other noise reduction or not, it sure looks like you did, and just a little too much of it too. Much of the natural "grain" of her features has been wiped away. It''s an interesting effect, but disturbing to many, myself included.<br/> <br/>Overall you did a fablulous job with this image. Like JM said, placing in the top 10 these days is no easy task. Congrats.</td>', 'You really did a great job with this image. Although I did not vote this challenge, I did look (I always look :) and this was one of the images that stood out.  The good things: Olivia''s awesome features. Seriously, she has the look a professional model. Unusual and just a touch exotic with some truely striking features. Non centered composition. It would have been so easy to plunk her into the center of the frame... but you didn''t. Kudos on that choice. Guitar earrings. Playful, and at the same time they echo the shape of her face and jawline.  The not so good: Sharpness is a double edged sword here. She is SO sharp (oversharp, in fact) that it is impossible not to comment on it. Portrait work is often suited to a mix of both sharpness and softness. You want sharpness on specific features, such as the eyes and lips, with softness on the others. Whether you actually used NI or other noise reduction or not, it sure looks like you did, and just a little too much of it too. Much of the natural "grain" of her features has been wiped away. It''s an interesting effect, but disturbing to many, myself included. Overall you did a fablulous job with this image. Like JM said, placing in the top 10 these days is no easy task. Congrats.', '2005-09-26 03:45:09.000000', NULL, 0),
